1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,547
An AI planner can be right about a world
that no longer exists.

2
00:00:05,547 --> 00:00:08,609
In a hospital, that world changes fast.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:14,227
GUARD sits between the planner and a
simulated robot, and only fresh,

4
00:00:14,227 --> 00:00:16,369
checked decisions can move it.

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,995
Here we kill the supervisor in the middle
of a run.

6
00:00:21,995 --> 00:00:24,971
With GUARD, the robot stops and latches.

7
00:00:24,971 --> 00:00:29,361
With a heartbeat-only baseline, the
planner is still alive,

8
00:00:29,361 --> 00:00:31,295
so the robot keeps moving.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,582
The planner only proposes motion.

10
00:00:42,582 --> 00:00:45,320
The supervisor grants short leases.

11
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:49,544
Once the supervisor is gone, nothing
renews the lease.

12
00:00:49,544 --> 00:00:54,864
So the gate, a separate process with its
own timer, stops the robot,

13
00:00:54,864 --> 00:00:58,619
and stays latched until an operator
recovers it.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,222
Now the MRI zone switches on, while the
planner still cites its old map.

15
00:01:15,222 --> 00:01:20,662
GUARD refuses the proposal with a reason
code, and the robot waits outside.

16
00:01:20,662 --> 00:01:25,884
Without a supervisor, the robot drives
straight through the active zone.

17
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:48,363
This is the same fault on QNX 8.0, running
in a QEMU virtual machine,

18
00:01:48,363 --> 00:01:51,252
with each part in its own process.

19
00:01:51,252 --> 00:01:53,546
guardctl injects the fault.

20
00:01:53,546 --> 00:01:57,880
The gate latches, and the cause is
supervisor lost.

21
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:12,290
Recovery is refused until its conditions
hold.

22
00:02:12,290 --> 00:02:15,334
Here we show behaviour, not timings.

23
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,287
Every number in our results comes from the
host Linux harness,

24
00:02:26,287 --> 00:02:28,016
and is labelled that way.

25
00:02:28,016 --> 00:02:33,479
GUARD is a simulation-only research
prototype, not for real robots or
patients.

26
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,700
Stale decisions can't move the robot.
